izzy: During a customer onboarding session today we hit a subtle Go SDK behavior: background workers that Stream() from Generator sometimes never terminate if the HTTP request that spawned them disconnects during an auth refresh. Repro with the onboarding sample app under 200 concurrent simulated clients.

izzy: Minimal repro pattern we used (deliberately naive):
```go
func wsHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
  go func() {
    ctx := r.Context()
    stream, err := client.Generator.Stream(ctx, req)
    if err != nil { return }
    for stream.Next() {
      // write tokens to websocket; client might disconnect mid-stream
    }
    // detached goroutine often exits without Close when client disconnects
  }()
  w.WriteHeader(101)
}
```

pat: Two issues: detached goroutine + assuming ctx cancel auto-closes underlying transport. When token rotation happens mid-request the SDK's internal send retry loop can block waiting for network ops, holding fds. We saw goroutines stuck in net.Conn.Write and in runtime.select.

kai: Recommending three immediate DX fixes: 1) Update sample app quickstart to show "proper" pattern (explicit cancel, drain, Close), 2) Add a go vet checker that flags detached goroutines using r.Context() with streaming calls, 3) Emit a debug metric open_generator_streams and a GC-finalizer warning when a Stream object is collected without Close.

izzy: For the quickstart we updated code live in the workshop to this pattern:
```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(r.Context())
defer cancel()
stream, err := client.Generator.Stream(ctx, req)
if err != nil { return }
defer stream.Close()
for stream.Next() {
  // forward tokens
}
// ensure drain to EOF before returning
```

pat: Also add an example using select so background workers stop cleanly if either the stream completes or the request cancels:
```go
for {
  select {
  case <-ctx.Done():
    stream.Close()
    return
  case <-stream.Done():
    return
  case tok := <-stream.Tokens():
    // handle
  }
}
```

kai: API discussion: make Stream implement io.Closer and expose Done()/Tokens() channels for ergonomics. But avoid breaking changes in a patch release. Proposal: add non-breaking methods Stream.Done() <-chan struct{} and CloseWithContext(ctx) error, keep existing methods, and document deprecation path for a v2. Add a runtime debug build tag that enables finalizer warnings.

izzy: Filed onboarding PR: devex/sample-apps#734 that updates quickstart and workshop notes. Also opened SDK RFC: sdk-go#118 "Generator stream ergonomics: Done/CloseWithContext and diagnostics".

pat: One more suggestion: auth/retry interaction seems to amplify leaks because transient retries extend lifetime. Add a short note in docs: "long-lived streaming background tasks should use explicit cancels; auth rotation can keep network ops active briefly".

release-bot: CI triggered for devex/sample-apps#734 (build 2112).

kai: I'll prototype the vet analyzer and add it to the local devtools repo. If it catches patterns we can gate it in CI for sample-apps and workshop repos.

izzy: Thanks — this should cut a lot of onboarding pain. I'll drop a short "Gotchas" snippet in the quickstart and the workshop slides. :thumbsup:
